WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.420
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.100
YUFENG GUO: What
does it take to build

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.850
a custom object recognition
mobile app starting

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.590
from raw data?

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:11.640
We'll find out on this episode
of "Cloud AI Adventures."

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:15.370
Today, we're joined by
Sara Robinson, a developer

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:19.050
advocate on the team who also
focuses on machine learning.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.150
She created an app
that recognizes

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.280
pictures of Taylor Swift.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.990
And she joins me
today in the studio

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.180
to discuss how she
accomplished it.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:27.870
Welcome, Sara.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:28.900
Thanks for joining me.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.025
SARA ROBINSON: Thanks for
having me-- super excited

00:00:31.025 --> 00:00:32.400
to be on "AI Adventures."

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.640
YUFENG GUO: So you wrote a
sensational medium article

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:40.230
that I've got open here about
your Swift Detector, which

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.930
kind of showcases
end-to-end machine learning,

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.810
from gathering and
pre-processing the data,

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:49.950
to training and serving from
Cloud Machine Learning Engine.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.436
And there's even Firebase
and an iPhone app in the mix.

00:00:52.436 --> 00:00:54.060
SARA ROBINSON: Lots
of different tools.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.268
YUFENG GUO: Yeah, definitely.

00:00:55.268 --> 00:00:56.310
It's super amazing.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:57.822
What inspired this project?

00:00:57.822 --> 00:00:59.280
SARA ROBINSON: So
I'd seen a couple

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.690
of other blog posts on the
TensorFlow Object Detection

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:02.190
API.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:03.030
And I'd been wanting to try it.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.160
Out these blog post had
things like detecting

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.010
pets, whether there is a
cat or a dog in an image.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:10.590
I saw another one about
detecting raccoons.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:11.640
YUFENG GUO: Oh, wow.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:12.150
SARA ROBINSON: So
then I thought,

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.670
I wanted to try it on my
own with some training

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:15.540
that I found.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.220
And the easiest
thing I thought of

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:19.050
was to just look
at my Google Photos

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.300
and find all the
pictures of myself

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:24.106
to train a model to recognize
me-- had lots of selfies.

00:01:24.106 --> 00:01:25.230
I had enough training data.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:26.430
YUFENG GUO: Yeah, and
that search function

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:28.220
on Google Photos, super helpful.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:29.597
SARA ROBINSON:
It's pretty great.

00:01:29.597 --> 00:01:30.930
So I trained that model locally.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.310
It works pretty well.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.170
But I wanted to build
an end-to-end demo.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:35.544
And I know a little
bit of Swift.

00:01:35.544 --> 00:01:38.040
So I thought would be cool if
I built a iOS client to make

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:41.069
a prediction request against
my model, which made me think,

00:01:41.069 --> 00:01:43.110
why don't I built a model
to detect Taylor Swift?

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:44.510
Because why not?

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:45.840
YUFENG GUO: Swift with Swift--

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:46.560
perfect.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.220
So it looks like here you're
using the TensorFlow Object

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:55.420
Detection API to find Taylor
Swift in these pictures.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.360
Can you talk a little
bit about what that is,

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.147
and what was it like to use it?

00:01:59.147 --> 00:02:00.230
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, sure.

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:03.240
So the Object Detection
API is some utilities

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.154
that make it easier to
recognize objects in an image.

00:02:06.154 --> 00:02:07.820
So rather than just
classifying an image

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.900
as this image has
a cat in it, it'll

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:13.830
return bounding boxes of where
that object is in a image.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:15.450
And to do that, it's
using a technique

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:19.050
called transfer learning, which
utilizes a model that's already

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.880
been trained on a
similar classification

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.030
task using millions
and millions of images.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.940
And then what I do as a
developer using Object

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:29.840
Detection API is I
take that checkpoints

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:32.160
of the second to
last layer of that

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.410
model that's been trained
on millions of images.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.270
And then I update
it with my own data,

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.250
so that I don't need
to start from scratch.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.350
So I didn't have time
to label millions

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:41.760
of images of Taylor Swift.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.230
YUFENG GUO: Though I'm sure you
wouldn't have minded, right?

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:44.400
SARA ROBINSON: I
wouldn't have minded

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.530
YUFENG GUO: Going through
all those pictures, yeah.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.530
SARA ROBINSON: So I just
needed to use a smaller

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:51.402
set of training data to update
the final layer of that model.

00:02:51.402 --> 00:02:53.610
YUFENG GUO: It's fantastic
that you can kind of stand

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:54.776
on the shoulders of giants--

00:02:54.776 --> 00:02:56.664
all the work and all
the training that's

00:02:56.664 --> 00:02:58.080
already been done
on these models.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.550
SARA ROBINSON: That's one of
the great things about transfer

00:02:59.550 --> 00:02:59.820
learning.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:00.528
YUFENG GUO: Yeah.

00:03:00.528 --> 00:03:02.710
So was the training kind
of quicker, as a result,

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:04.350
because you didn't have to
go through as many images?

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:05.290
SARA ROBINSON:
Mm-hmm, much faster.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:08.080
So instead of taking maybe days,
it took just a couple of hours.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:09.371
YUFENG GUO: Fantastic, awesome.

00:03:09.371 --> 00:03:12.960
And then so actually
doing the work that--

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:16.770
I guess you would have had
to somehow get the images,

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:22.950
and do any pre-processing
required to have the TensorFlow

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:25.420
Object Detection API kind of
consume those images, what

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:29.540
formatting you needed to do, and
then actually run the training.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:30.480
SARA ROBINSON: Mm-hmm.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.660
So the Object Detection
API requires your images

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:35.500
be in what's called
a TFRecord format--

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:37.020
YUFENG GUO: TFRecord, OK.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.030
SARA ROBINSON: And to
get them in that format,

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:41.892
I converted my images to
what's called the Pascal VOC

00:03:41.892 --> 00:03:44.100
format, which is just one
format you can choose from.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.440
It stands for Visual
Object Classification.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.280
And what that is is
that's an XML file.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.700
So I take all my images and
I generate this XML file,

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.020
which gives me details on
the bounding box of where

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:56.804
the object is.

00:03:56.804 --> 00:03:58.470
YUFENG GUO: So that's
what's in the XML.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:02.174
It's just kind of coordinates
here of the boxes themselves.

00:04:02.174 --> 00:04:03.090
SARA ROBINSON: Mm-hmm.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:06.272
So it has the x,y coordinates of
where my object is is an image.

00:04:06.272 --> 00:04:07.980
And then it just has
the label associated

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.600
with that bounding box.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.787
In this case, I just had
one label, Taylor Swift.

00:04:11.787 --> 00:04:12.870
YUFENG GUO: Only need one.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.407
SARA ROBINSON: I only need one.

00:04:14.407 --> 00:04:15.240
YUFENG GUO: Awesome.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:16.870
So let's see.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:18.079
We have our data.

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:19.089
It's labeled.

00:04:19.089 --> 00:04:22.170
And I guess now there's only one
thing to do, hit the training.

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:24.120
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah,
so training is next.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.220
YUFENG GUO: So with all
these kind of steps,

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:30.494
can you just outline in how
they all connect together?

00:04:30.494 --> 00:04:31.410
SARA ROBINSON: Mm-hmm.

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:34.170
So I drew about bounding
boxes around my images,

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:36.529
got them into the Pascal format.

00:04:36.529 --> 00:04:38.070
And then once they're
in that format,

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.730
I wrote a script to convert them
a TFRecord, which I can then

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:43.309
use to feed into my model.

00:04:43.309 --> 00:04:44.100
YUFENG GUO: Gotcha.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.510
OK, so all this kind of steps,
now they're all connected.

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:49.780
You've gone from
the raw image to a--

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:52.990
well, not just a labeled image,
but specifically, TFRecords.

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:54.880
It's even formatted correctly.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:57.770
So now we're ready for our
training, I guess, right?

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:01.137
And so in this case, we're going
your training on ML Engine,

00:05:01.137 --> 00:05:01.720
it looks like.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:04.080
It's on your blog post here.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:05.450
How is that process?

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:07.367
And what did you have
to do to make that work?

00:05:07.367 --> 00:05:09.324
SARA ROBINSON: So it was
similar to the process

00:05:09.324 --> 00:05:11.280
I used for running
the training locally.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:13.613
The main difference being I
needed to put all of my data

00:05:13.613 --> 00:05:16.994
in Google Cloud Storage and
tell ML Engine where to find it.

00:05:16.994 --> 00:05:19.410
So the first thing I needed
to get in Google Cloud Storage

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:22.530
was the model checkpoints
from the pre-trained model

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:23.160
I was using.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.440
I used a model called
MobileNets, which is optimized

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.450
for mobile, obviously.

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:30.540
So I put those checkpoints
in my Cloud Storage bucket.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:33.930
I also needed my training
and test TFRecord files,

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:36.490
which I talked about before
how I generated those.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:38.430
And then I have a
config file, which

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:41.670
tells the Object Detection
API where my training and test

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:42.480
data is located.

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:45.879
And it supports ML Engine
and Google Cloud Storage.

00:05:45.879 --> 00:05:46.920
YUFENG GUO: Great, great.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:51.271
And so that whole thing kind
of comes together and, I guess,

00:05:51.271 --> 00:05:53.145
just takes care of all
training for you then?

00:05:53.145 --> 00:05:53.430
SARA ROBINSON: Mm-hmm.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:53.730
YUFENG GUO: Awesome.

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:55.480
Then how long did that
training time take?

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.590
Everyone wants to know
if the training takes

00:05:57.590 --> 00:05:59.876
a long time, or a little
time, and how long you

00:05:59.876 --> 00:06:00.750
had to wait for that.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:01.930
SARA ROBINSON: I think
it took about 30 minutes.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:03.120
YUFENG GUO: 30 minutes,
OK, that's not too bad.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:03.730
SARA ROBINSON: Not too long.

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:05.830
YUFENG GUO: Yeah, given what
you're getting out of it,

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:06.690
that's quite nice.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:07.390
SARA ROBINSON: Exactly.

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:08.931
YUFENG GUO: So once
training is done,

00:06:08.931 --> 00:06:10.910
what do you have kind
of as your result?

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:11.690
What do you have in your hand?

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:13.606
SARA ROBINSON: So you
have a model checkpoint,

00:06:13.606 --> 00:06:14.850
which is just a binary file.

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:16.920
Similar to the pre-trained
model checkpoint

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:18.684
that I used to
start training, this

00:06:18.684 --> 00:06:20.100
is now going to
be the final model

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.320
checkpoint, which
has been updated

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:24.312
with all of my training data.

00:06:24.312 --> 00:06:25.270
YUFENG GUO: Gotcha, OK.

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:26.240
So that's very nice.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:28.560
And then I guess
we now kind of flip

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:30.790
it over the wall to
the serving side.

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:33.940
SARA ROBINSON: So to serve
it, I can use gcloud command

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:35.550
with ML Engine to
deploy my model.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:38.700
I just need to point it at
where those checkpoint files are

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.609
of my final trained model.

00:06:40.609 --> 00:06:42.150
And then in just a
couple of minutes,

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:44.280
I have my model
available for serving.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:47.590
And I can use that to generate
predictions against the model.

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:49.000
YUFENG GUO: Fantastic.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.390
And so I guess this
kind of brings us

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:54.945
to the next phase
of this project.

00:06:54.945 --> 00:06:56.910
Here we've gone from our data.

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:58.440
We've pre-processed it.

00:06:58.440 --> 00:06:59.910
We've done the training.

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:04.940
And we have this kind
of API that can serve up

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:06.250
predictions at scale.

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:07.208
SARA ROBINSON: Exactly.

00:07:07.208 --> 00:07:09.690
YUFENG GUO: So now we're
ready to call that endpoint.

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:12.240
And what did you end up
kind of architecting here?

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.144
It looks like you have
a couple of pieces.

00:07:14.144 --> 00:07:15.060
SARA ROBINSON: Mm-hmm.

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.970
It's using a couple of
different Firebase SDKs.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:20.580
So the client is a Swift app.

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:22.800
It's an iOS app that's
written in Swift.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:24.550
And it's a pretty thin client.

00:07:24.550 --> 00:07:26.730
So all the client is
doing is it's uploading

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:29.030
an image to Firebase Storage.

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:31.262
YUFENG GUO: Oh, OK, so that's
what that arrow is for.

00:07:31.262 --> 00:07:32.220
SARA ROBINSON: Exactly.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:34.680
And then we're using
the Firebase SDK

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.060
for Cloud Functions.

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.460
So a function is triggered
whenever an image is uploaded

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:40.190
to a specific storage bucket.

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:42.102
YUFENG GUO: Ah, very handy.

00:07:42.102 --> 00:07:44.310
SARA ROBINSON: So the function
is written in Node.js.

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:46.435
And that function does a
bunch of different things.

00:07:46.435 --> 00:07:47.730
It downloads the image.

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:48.960
Then it resizes it.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:53.790
It base64 encodes it, which will
prepare it for the ML Engine

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:54.995
online prediction request.

00:07:54.995 --> 00:07:55.620
YUFENG GUO: OK.

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:57.780
SARA ROBINSON: So I make
that prediction request.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:00.480
If the confidence that I get
back is greater than 70%--

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.870
that's just a threshold
that I've chosen--

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:04.970
I'm going to draw a
box around that image.

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:06.810
YUFENG GUO: OK,
in the box drawing

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:09.302
is it looks like it's happening
here in your Node code.

00:08:09.302 --> 00:08:11.010
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah,
I'm doing that using

00:08:11.010 --> 00:08:13.920
ImageMagick, which is available
in Google Cloud Functions.

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:16.430
YUFENG GUO: Oh, perfect,
very convenient.

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:20.160
And so then, once
the box is drawn,

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:23.020
how do you get that information
back to your client then?

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:24.686
SARA ROBINSON: So
once the box is drawn,

00:08:24.686 --> 00:08:26.460
I write the image
to Firebase Storage.

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:29.100
And then I also write
some metadata on the image

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:32.490
to Cloud Firestore,
which is a database.

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:35.640
So I'm writing the
confidence of the detection

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:38.000
along with the path of the
image in Firebase Storage.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:38.791
YUFENG GUO: Gotcha.

00:08:38.791 --> 00:08:41.610
So that's why you kind of have
these two write operations

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:42.961
coming from your Cloud Function?

00:08:42.961 --> 00:08:43.919
SARA ROBINSON: Exactly.

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:46.140
And the reason I'm using
Firestore is, in my client,

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:50.250
I can create a listener on
the ID of that image path.

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:52.560
And so then I get
updates whenever

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:55.740
new data has been added to
that collection in Firestore

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:59.111
so I don't have to keep pulling
that to see if the detection is

00:08:59.111 --> 00:08:59.610
finished.

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:02.026
So right when it's finished,
my client will get an update.

00:09:02.026 --> 00:09:04.890
And then I can then
download the new image

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:07.980
with the box around it, along
with the confidence detection

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:08.765
score.

00:09:08.765 --> 00:09:10.140
YUFENG GUO: So
that's really neat

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:14.730
that you've managed to use
the kind of architecture

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:17.220
to signal in both directions.

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:20.250
You upload to Cloud
Storage, and that

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:21.420
triggers the Cloud Function.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:23.610
So on upload, something happens.

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:25.240
And it happens right away.

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:27.930
And then coming back, once
the response from ML Engine

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:31.050
